        },
        "elapsedMs": 18962,
        "text": "Absolutely. You mentioned in our earlier DM that you enjoy **Premiere Pro** and are learning its **Lumetri Color panel**, so I’d start with these:\n\n### Free resources\n- **[Adobe Premiere tutorials](https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere-pro/tutorials.html)** — Official lessons on cutting clips, audio, titles, color correction, and exporting. A reliable starting point for learning the tools.\n- **[Premiere Gal](https://www.youtube.com/@PremiereGal)** — Practical Premiere tutorials covering editing workflows, captions, effects, and audio. Great for following along with a small project.\n- **[This Guy Edits](https://www.youtube.com/@ThisGuyEdits)** — Focuses on *why* an edit works: pacing, emotion, shot selection, and storytelling, rather than just software buttons.\n- **[Frame.io Insider](https://blog.frame.io/)** — Detailed articles on editing workflows, codecs, color, and post-production. Useful when you want to understand the technical side.\n\n### Structured learning and books\n- **LinkedIn Learning** — Look for a recent *Premiere Pro Essential Training* course. It’s paid, but some public libraries offer free access.\n- **Adobe Premiere Pro Classroom in a Book** — Guided exercises with project files; choose an edition that matches your software version.\n- **In the Blink of an Eye by Walter Murch** — A short, thoughtful book about choosing where to cut and how editing shapes a viewer’s experience. Not software-specific.\n\n**My suggested starting exercise:** Make a 30–60-second video from your own footage. Focus first on clean cuts and clear sound, then use Lumetri to adjust white balance and exposure and match your shots. One finished project will teach you more than watching a dozen tutorials without practising."
      },
